2.2.m:
Now then begins the procreation of ṛc and sāman:
When he utters the hiṅkāra, he roars toward [her] by that;
when he chants the prastāva, he leaps onto [her] by that;
when he takes up the ādi, he emits semen by that;
when he chants the udgītha, he causes the emitted semen to develop by that;
when he chants the pratihāra, he makes the developed semen grow by that;
when he chants the upadrava, he gives form to the grown semen by that;
when he reaches the nidhana, he brings forth the formed semen by that;
this is the procreation of ṛc and sāman.

2.3.m:
They desired to reach it through speech;
they mounted speech;
he took hold around their speech;
therefore speech is taken hold of around;
for with it one speaks both truth and untruth.

They desired to reach it through mind;
they mounted mind;
he took hold around their mind;
therefore mind is taken hold of around;
for with it one thinks both good and evil.

They desired to reach it through sight;
they mounted sight;
he took hold around their sight;
therefore sight is taken hold of around;
for with it one sees both what is worthy to be seen and what is unworthy to be seen.

They desired to reach it through hearing;
they mounted hearing;
he took hold around their hearing;
therefore hearing is taken hold of around;
for with it one hears both what is worthy to be heard and what is unworthy to be heard.

They desired to reach it through downward breath;
they mounted downward breath;
he took hold around their downward breath;
therefore downward breath is taken hold of around;
for with it one smells both fragrant and foul odors.

2.4.m:
'Origin' (ābhūti), say the Kārīrādayas;
for following breath, offspring and cattle come into being;
he who thus venerates this as 'origin' becomes [plentiful] with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Union' (sambhūti), says Sātyayajñi;
for following breath, offspring and cattle unite;
he who thus venerates this as 'union' thrives with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Power' (prabhūti), say the Śailanas;
for following breath, offspring and cattle come forth;
he who thus venerates this as 'power' comes forth indeed with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Being' (bhūti), say the Bhāllabins;
for following breath, offspring and cattle are;
he who thus venerates this as 'being' is indeed [rich] in breath, offspring, and cattle.

'The excluded one, unexcluded,' says Pārṣṇa Śailana;
for this excludes the other, another does not exclude this;
he excludes his hating rival who knows thus.
Second khaṇḍa in the second anuvāka.

2.5.m:
'The sole hero,' says Āruṇeya;
for breath is the only hero;
one heroic, vigorous son is born to him who knows thus.

2.8.m:
The asuras said: “Come, let us find out who this is that has treated us thus.”
They came thither;
having approached, they saw him.

3.2.m:
"Gold-toothed, clean, not born a son":
for he is not a son;
though he has the form of a son, he is not a son.

3.3.m:
Then Vasiṣṭha approached him [saying], "This uktha is the cow (go)."
That is just food;
for the cow is food.

3.5.m:
Having approached the bahiṣpavamāna, he should breathe in [with the sound] "ṭītra viyi", and breathe out with speech [saying] "ṭītra gṛhitra".
He should desire to see with both eyes; he should desire to hear with both ears;
this [chant] is spontaneously yoked to the mind.

3.9.m:
"Then, when they consecrate him, then he dies for the second time.
They shave his hair and beard;
they cut his nails;
they anoint his limbs;
he bends in his fingers;
he sits uncovered and wrapped up;
he does not offer oblation;
he does not sacrifice;
he does not approach a woman;
he speaks non-human speech;
he is then indeed in the form of a dead man.

3.10.m:
"Indeed, sir, your father considered him an udgātṛ," he said.
And Prācīnaśāla they know as the one who was their chosen udgātṛ;
concerning that, he did not know.

3.11.m:
He dies this first [death] when the semen emitted becomes unified;
he enters into breath;
he is born into hope.

Then he dies this second [death] when he is consecrated;
he enters into the metres;
he is born into the sacrificial fee.

Then he dies this third [death] when he dies;
he enters into faith;
he is born into the world.

Therefore he sings this gāyatra chant in three turns.
With its first turn, he wins this very world and whatever is in this world;
thereby he enriches him with that breath into which he enters, and bestows upon him that hope into which he is born.

Then with the second turn, he wins this intermediate space and whatever is in the intermediate space;
thereby he enriches him with those metres into which he enters,
and bestows upon him that sacrificial fee into which he is born.

3.13.m:
And regarding this, Śāṭyāyani said: 'For what desire would he fix a post?
This is merely accompanying singing;
he should pay no heed to this.'

Whoever, being wingless, goes to the top of a tree, falls down from there;
but when a winged [bird] sits on a treetop, on the edge of a sword, on the edge of a razor, it does not fall down from there,
for it sits held firmly by its two wings.

3.14.m:
On that self there is a falling back;
the seasons drag him down, seized by his feet together;
day and night obtain his world.

He enters this very essence of good deeds.
Whatever human beings sacrifice in this world, whatever good they do, their food-essence rises upward;
it enters that moon, the world of human beings.

This human-resembling egg forms within the belly;
its food-essence rises upward toward the breasts;
when he is born, then the mother offers him the breast, the food-essence.

A man is indeed unborn as long as he does not sacrifice;
he is born only through the sacrifice;
just like an egg broken open for the first time, even so is it.

3.16.m:
With speech, the hotṛ, the adhvaryu, and the udgātṛ prepare one track of it;
therefore they perform with speech.
The brahman priest alone [prepares] the other [track] with mind;
therefore he sits silently.

3.17.m:
Concerning this there is this verse:
'In me I think is this all, beginning with the world; in me are the worlds, in me the four quarters;
In me I think is this which blinks and moves; in me are the waters and all the plants.'
